#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:3
msgid "Benchmarking"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:5
msgid ""
"CodeIgniter provides two separate tools to help you benchmark your code "
"and test different options: the **Timer** and the **Iterator**. The Timer"
" allows you to easily calculate the time between two points in the "
"execution of your script. The Iterator allows you to set up several "
"variations and runs those tests, recording performance and memory "
"statistics to help you decide which version is the best."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:10
msgid ""
"The Timer class is always active, being started from the moment the "
"framework is invoked until right before sending the output to the user, "
"enabling a very accurate timing of the entire system execution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:19
msgid "Using the Timer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:21
msgid ""
"With the Timer, you can measure the time between two moments in the "
"execution of your application. This makes it simple to measure the "
"performance of different aspects of your application. All measurement is "
"done using the ``start()`` and ``stop()`` methods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:26
msgid "Timer::start()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:28
msgid ""
"The ``start()`` method takes a single parameter: the name of this timer. "
"You can use any string as the name of the timer. It is only used for you "
"to reference later to know which measurement is which:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:34
msgid "Timer::stop()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:36
msgid ""
"The ``stop()`` method takes the name of the timer that you want to stop "
"as the only parameter, also:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:40
msgid ""
"The name is not case-sensitive, but otherwise must match the name you "
"gave it when you started the timer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:43
msgid "timer()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:45
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can use the :doc:`global function "
"</general/common_functions>` ``timer()`` to start and stop timers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:53
msgid "Timer::record()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:57
msgid ""
"Since v4.3.0, if you use very small code blocks to benchmark, you can "
"also use the ``record()`` method. It accepts a no-parameter callable and "
"measures its execution time. Methods ``start()`` and ``stop()`` will be "
"called automatically around the function call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:63
msgid "You can also return the callable's return value for further processing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:67
msgid ""
"The same functionality is also available when passing callable to "
"``timer()`` as second parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:72
msgid "Viewing Your Benchmark Points"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:74
msgid ""
"When your application runs, all of the timers that you have set are "
"collected by the Timer class. It does not automatically display them, "
"though. You can retrieve all of your timers by calling the "
"``getTimers()`` method. This returns an array of benchmark information, "
"including start, end, and duration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:80
msgid ""
"You can change the precision of the calculated duration by passing in the"
" number of decimal places you want to be shown as the only parameter. The"
" default value is 4 numbers behind the decimal point:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:85
msgid ""
"The timers are automatically displayed in the :doc:`Debug Toolbar "
"</testing/debugging>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:88
msgid "Displaying Execution Time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:90
msgid ""
"While the ``getTimers()`` method will give you the raw data for all of "
"the timers in your project, you can retrieve the duration of a single "
"timer, in seconds, with the ``getElapsedTime()`` method. The first "
"parameter is the name of the timer to display. The second is the number "
"of decimal places to display. This defaults to 4:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:98
msgid "Using the Iterator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:100
msgid ""
"The Iterator is a simple tool that is designed to allow you to try out "
"multiple variations on a solution to see the speed differences and "
"different memory usage patterns. You can add any number of \"tasks\" for "
"it to run and the class will run the task hundreds or thousands of times "
"to get a clearer picture of performance. The results can then be "
"retrieved and used by your script, or displayed as an HTML table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:106
msgid "Creating Tasks To Run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:108
msgid ""
"Tasks are defined within Closures. Any output the task creates will be "
"discarded automatically. They are added to the Iterator class through the"
" ``add()`` method. The first parameter is a name you want to refer to "
"this test by. The second parameter is the Closure, itself:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:115
msgid "Running the Tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:117
msgid ""
"Once you've added the tasks to run, you can use the ``run()`` method to "
"loop over the tasks many times. By default, it will run each task 1000 "
"times. This is probably sufficient for most simple tests. If you need to "
"run the tests more times than that, you can pass the number as the first "
"parameter:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/testing/benchmark.rst:123
msgid ""
"Once it has run, it will return an HTML table with the results of the "
"test. If you don't want the results, you can pass in ``false`` as the "
"second parameter:"
msgstr ""

